\details{
Each workbook states the scoring rules twice: as runnable SPSS and Stata
syntax, and as a printed score table on the \emph{Other software} tab. This
function takes the \strong{syntax} as authoritative, because it maps a response
\emph{code} to a score with no ambiguity, and uses the \emph{Other software} tab only
for option labels and as an independent cross-check.

When the two disagree the difference is recorded in the object (see
\code{\link[=et_checks]{et_checks()}}) and, if \code{verbose = TRUE}, reported as a warning. This is not
hypothetical: in the published Cambodia DHS 2021 workbook the national block
of the \emph{Other software} tab lists the \code{Q8} response options in a different
order from the syntax tabs, so the two documented routes give different
scores for the same household.

Three structural variants occur in the published corpus and all are handled:
\itemize{
\item \code{"national_urban"} -- separate national and urban score sets (most tools).
\item \code{"rural_urban"} -- separate rural and urban score sets that are mapped onto
a common national scale by a linear equation. Scoring on the national scale
therefore requires an urban/rural indicator; see the \code{area} argument of
\code{\link[=et_score]{et_score()}}.
\item \code{"national_only"} -- national scores with no urban tool.
}
